# Expense Reimbursement Policy — defined terms

> Module ExpenseDefs

This module holds the defined terms of E-2 and nothing else. It decides no
entitlement: `MealPerDiem`, `Accommodation`, `GroundTransport` and
`ClaimWindow` each consume the types and the two computations declared here.

Two of the four E-2 definitions (E-2.1, E-2.2) are exhausted by the *shape* of
the data they name, so their encoding is a type declaration and there is no
rule to state. The other two (E-2.3, E-2.4) each reduce to a computation — a
day count and a three-limbed predicate — and those are scopes.

## Prologue — declarations

```catala-metadata
declaration enumeration CityTier:
  -- Tier1
  -- Tier2
  -- Tier3

declaration structure ClientBillable:
  data is_client_billable content boolean
  data sow_per_diem content optional of money

declaration structure PreApproval:
  data obtained content boolean
  data in_writing content boolean
  data obtained_at content date
  data approver_authority_limit content money
  data specified_amount content optional of money

declaration scope TravelDayCount:
  input departure_date content date
  input return_date content date
  output travel_days content integer

declaration scope PreApprovalValid:
  input pre_approval content PreApproval
  input expense_incurred_at content date
  input expense_amount content money
  output valid content boolean
```

`PreApproval.obtained` exists so that the struct can represent *no approval at
all* without inventing a meaningless `obtained_at`. It adds no law: with
`obtained` false the E-2.4 predicate below is false whatever the other fields
say.

`PreApproval.specified_amount` is the amount the approval was given for. E-2.4
itself does not mention an amount, but E-3.3 turns on "a specified higher
amount" and E-2.4 turns on the approver's authority being "at or above the
threshold of the expense", so the approved figure has to travel with the
approval.

## E-2.1 City Tier

| EXP-POL E-2.1 (002-expense-reimbursement-policy.md:28)
|
| "City Tier" means the tier assigned to the city in which the
| expenditure is incurred, as published in the Finance intranet schedule, being
| Tier 1, Tier 2 or Tier 3.

E-2.1 is encoded by the `CityTier` enumeration in the prologue. Its whole
computable content is that the tier is one of exactly three values: the
assignment of a city to a tier is a lookup in a schedule published outside this
corpus, so it reaches every scope as an input. Because the enumeration is
closed, Catala's exhaustiveness check guarantees that the E-3.1 and E-4.1
tables cover every tier — which is the reason this clause is RULE and not
PROSE.

There is no rule to state beyond the type, so this clause has no code block of
its own.

## E-2.2 Client-Billable Travel

| EXP-POL E-2.2 (002-expense-reimbursement-policy.md:32)
|
| "Client-Billable Travel" means travel which is recoverable from a
| client under the terms of an executed statement of work.

E-2.2 is encoded by the `ClientBillable` structure in the prologue. Whether
travel is recoverable from a client is a term of the executed statement of
work, so it is a recorded fact and an input. The optional `sow_per_diem` is the
second thing the statement of work may supply, and E-3.2 switches on whether it
is `Present` or `Absent`. As with E-2.1 the clause contributes a type and no
arithmetic, so it has no code block.

## E-2.3 Travel Day

| EXP-POL E-2.3 (002-expense-reimbursement-policy.md:35)
|
| "Travel Day" means each calendar day, or part of a calendar day, on
| which the Employee is away from their principal place of work overnight on
| Company business. The day of departure and the day of return are each a Travel
| Day.

The count is inclusive at both ends: the second sentence puts the day of
departure and the day of return *each* inside the count, so a trip leaving on
the 10th and returning on the 12th is three Travel Days, not two. Writing this
as `return - departure` would drop one day and understate every per diem by one
day's cap, which is why the clause is encoded as a rule rather than left to
each caller.

The first sentence requires the Employee to be away **overnight**. A departure
and a return on the same calendar day involve no night away, so no Travel Day
arises; the two limbs are grouped under one label because they are mutually
exclusive readings of the same clause, not an exception to one another.

```catala
scope TravelDayCount:
  label e2_3 definition travel_days
    under condition return_date <= departure_date
    consequence equals 0

  label e2_3 definition travel_days
    under condition return_date > departure_date
    consequence equals
      integer of ((return_date - departure_date) / (1 day)) + 1
```

## E-2.4 Pre-Approval

| EXP-POL E-2.4 (002-expense-reimbursement-policy.md:40)
|
| "Pre-Approval" means written approval obtained before the expense is
| incurred from a person holding delegated financial authority at or above the
| threshold of the expense.

Three conjuncts, each decidable: the approval is in writing; it was obtained
before the expense was incurred (a date comparison); and the approver's
delegated authority is at or above the threshold of the expense (a numeric
comparison, `>=`, because the clause says "at or above"). This is why E-3.3 and
E-5.2 are RULE and not HYBRID — nothing here is a judgement.

| NOTE: "obtained before the expense is incurred" is encoded as a strict
| comparison of *dates*, which is the finest granularity the corpus offers.
| An approval given earlier on the same calendar day as the expense was in fact
| obtained before the expense, but at date granularity it is indistinguishable
| from one given afterwards, and this encoding treats it as too late. The
| alternative — treating same-day approval as valid — would let an approval
| obtained after the spend qualify. Resolving this needs a timestamp the corpus
| does not define.

```catala
scope PreApprovalValid:
  definition valid equals
    pre_approval.obtained
    and pre_approval.in_writing
    and pre_approval.obtained_at < expense_incurred_at
    and pre_approval.approver_authority_limit >= expense_amount
```
